LR  - 20251203
IS  - 2694-0604 (Electronic)
IS  - 2375-7477 (Linking)
VI  - 2025
DP  - 2025 Jul
TI  - A Natural Language Processing Model Based on Anamnesis Reports to Predict Acute 
      Myocardial Infarction Risk in Emergency Chest Pain Patients.
PG  - 1-4
LID - 10.1109/EMBC58623.2025.11253291 [doi]
AB  - This work is focused on predicting the risk of acute myocardial infarction (AMI) 
      by analysing textual data from anamnesis reports (patient's personal medical 
      history) to improve risk stratification in emergency departments. A natural 
      language processing (NLP) model was fine-tuned using pre-trained algorithms and 
      the European Society of Cardiology (ESC) 0-1h algorithm was employed to evaluate 
      two troponin measurements, determining the endpoint as either rule-out or rule-in 
      for AMI. The analysed dataset comprised patients with chest pain treated at the 
      emergency department of the Hospital Clínico Universitario de Valencia 
      (Spain).The results demonstrated that the model achieved an impressive accuracy 
      of 0.975, with a sensitivity of 1.0 and a specificity of 0.95 when incorporating 
      the first troponin measurement alongside the text from anamnesis reports. Models 
      relying solely on textual data without troponin information showed limited 
      clinical applicability.Clinical Relevance- This work underscores the significant 
      potential of natural language processing (NLP) as a diagnostic support tool, 
      particularly considering the substantial volume of patient information stored in 
      textual format. The proposed model facilitates more precise identification of 
      high-risk patients, enabling timely clinical interventions and optimizing 
      resource allocation in emergency departments. Furthermore, the integration of a 
      critical biomarker, such as the initial troponin measurement, enhances the 
      reliability of the model, ensuring greater robustness and applicability for its 
      implementation in routine clinical practice.
