PMID- 41086266
STAT- Publisher
PB  - Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute (PCORI)
CTI - PCORI Final Research Reports
DP  - 2023 Jul
BTI - Developing and Testing Software for Extracting COVID-19–Related Information from 
      Clinical Notes in Electronic Health Records
AB  - BACKGROUND: COVID-19 is caused by the SARS-CoV-2 virus and was first detected in 
      Wuhan, China, and reported to the World Health Organization Country Office in 
      China on December 31, 2019. It was declared a public health emergency of 
      international concern on January 30, 2020, and a pandemic on March 11, 2020. The 
      first COVID-19 case in the United States was confirmed on January 21, 2020. A 
      rapid expansion to all 50 US states was observed, with approximately 47 500 000 
      confirmed cases and almost 770 000 deaths as of November 15, 2021. Detailed 
      clinical information is needed to help assess the extent of the pandemic, 
      characterize the virus and the disease it causes, discover and compare supportive 
      or therapeutic approaches, and apply population health measures at the patient 
      level. This detailed information is typically found in unstructured text notes 
      from electronic health record (EHR) systems or other ancillary systems. 
      Extracting such information manually is costly, not scalable, and far too slow to 
      address current needs. OBJECTIVES: Our general objective was to develop and 
      assess a new software application for COVID-19-related information extraction 
      based on the same new natural language processing (NLP) methods to improve the 
      accuracy, validity, and efficiency of information extraction from EHR text 
      proposed in our current PCORI award. We proposed the following aims: (1) Develop 
      a new reference standard of clinical text notes annotated with COVID-19-related 
      information by using new assisted annotation methods to minimize clinical note 
      annotation burdens with novel active learning algorithms that leverage feature 
      expansion and semisupervised learning and (2) develop and assess a new NLP-based 
      tool for accurate COVID-19-related information extraction. METHODS: As a solution 
      to help accurately extract detailed clinical information related to COVID-19 from 
      clinical notes, we developed and assessed a software application based on NLP and 
      novel methods and tools proposed in our current PCORI award. This COVID-19 NLP 
      tool is our main deliverable for the enhancement grant. A new reference standard 
      (sometimes called a gold standard and commonly used for evaluating system 
      performance) of clinical text notes annotated with COVID-19-related information 
      was created. An annotation guideline was developed to define all COVID-19-related 
      information to annotate and then extract from clinical notes. This annotation 
      guideline will be released via GitHub, along with other annotation guidelines 
      that we have developed, at https://github.com/MUSC-TBIC/annotation_schemas. The 
      guideline is also available in Appendix A. The INCEpTION annotation tool was 
      adapted and used as a secure web application for clinical note annotation with 
      COVID-19-related information. The annotated collection includes 75 clinical notes 
      from both institutions (ie, the Medical University of South Carolina and 
      Vanderbilt University). We built the new NLP-based tool (named DECOVRI for Data 
      Extraction for COVID-19-Related Information) on a standard framework based on 
      Apache UIMA (Unstructured Information Management Architecture) and used 
      open-source resources to ease dissemination. The current release of DECOVRI 
      includes preprocessing modules for accurate detection and classification of 
      sections and splitting of text into sentences and tokens (ie, words). To extract 
      COVID-19-related information, we combined 3 major methods for finding and 
      extracting concepts from unstructured notes: a dictionary lookup and 2 machine 
      learning methods (specifically, conditional random fields and bidirectional long 
      short-term memory deep neural networks). Our tool also performs local context 
      analysis of all extracted information to determine whether the extracted 
      information is currently true and about the patient (as opposed to historical 
      data, negated data, and data about someone other than the patient). It exports 
      the results to a common data model, the Observational Medical Outcomes 
      Partnership. Common data models define standards for stored data and 
      relationships across those data, which facilitates sharing of the results with 
      other investigations and institutions. To assess the accuracy of DECOVRI, the 
      testing subset from the reference standard was used and the tool output compared 
      with the reference standard annotations. Note: UMLS licensing constraints prevent 
      direct release of the lexicons used in this project. Instructions for generating 
      project dictionaries for lexical lookup are available via DECOVRI's GitHub page 
      and can be used in tandem with lex_gen, a freely-available tool developed by our 
      team. RESULTS: The study population was defined in detail. Clinical notes for 
      this population were selected to be included in the reference standard. The 
      annotation guideline was developed and implemented in a secure web-based 
      annotation tool at both sites. The annotation process by domain experts was 
      delayed but eventually completed with good intermediary interannotator 
      measurements. The new NLP tool, DECOVRI, was developed and first released in May 
      2020 as a beta version including the preprocessing and dictionary lookup modules. 
      An initial accuracy evaluation based on a small set of 15 clinical notes resulted 
      in a micro-averaged F(1)-measure of 0.7254 and a macro-averaged F(1)-measure of 
      0.8316. The F(1)-measure is the harmonic mean of recall and precision, with equal 
      weight given to both. The micro-average collapses averages at the item level, 
      which emphasizes item frequency regardless of class; and the macro-average pools 
      at the class level, which emphasizes equitable performance across classes. The 
      higher macro- than micro-average indicates strong performance across classes and 
      weaker performance on higher frequency classes. The full version of DECOVRI, 
      including machine learning and more advanced components, was released in October 
      2021. Both versions are available at https://github.com/MUSC-TBIC. In short, 
      DECOVRI was developed and released as a tool available to other researchers and 
      institutions to extract COVID-19-related signs and symptoms for downstream uses 
      such as automated symptom monitoring or cohort creation. Performance, based on a 
      sample data set created during this grant, indicates DECOVRI performs better 
      across a wide range of concepts than on just the most frequent concepts. 
      CONCLUSIONS: Despite delays in the development of a new reference standard of 
      clinical notes, an initial prototype version of a novel NLP tool was developed in 
      record time and then progressively improved with new or revised components, 
      allowing for consistent and accurate information extraction. LIMITATIONS: The 
      small size of the corpus used for the accuracy evaluation results in limited 
      precision of the accuracy metrics measured.
